The battery gauge said 0% and the phone shut itself down

WHAT HAPPENED
-------------
On 2026-08-22, in the middle of a work session, the phone shut itself down. It
was not the cable: `qcom_qg` was reporting capacity 0 with the cell at 3.57 V
-which in lithium is 25-30%- and UPower presented it as

    state:      empty
    percentage: 0

that is, a flat battery, hence the critical battery action. And the worst part is
that it feeds back on itself: when powering up from off, the gauge reads the same
thing again.

THE TWO CAUSES
--------------
1. THE CAPACITY IS COMPUTED FROM A DIFFERENT REGISTER THAN THE VOLTAGE.

   `qcom_qg_get_capacity()` interpolates linearly between the design voltages
   -the driver itself admits it: "Yes, this function simply calculates the
   capacity based on the current voltage"- but it reads the S2 AVERAGE (0x80),
   whereas the sysfs `voltage_now` property reads the ADC's LAST SAMPLE (0xc0).
   That is why this was seen, which looks contradictory and is not:

       capacity    = 0
       voltage_now = 3565555     (would give 16% with the same formula)

   The average is accumulated by the hardware. After a COLD boot it does not have
   samples yet and reads zero, and zero translates to 0%. Verified that a
   `reboot` does NOT reproduce it: the PMIC does not power down and the average
   survives -measured, `voltage_avg` identical byte for byte before and after
   rebooting-. That is why the symptom only appeared after the power-off.

2. THE STATUS WAS ALWAYS UNKNOWN.

       case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_STATUS:
       	val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_STATUS_UNKNOWN;

   Without looking at anything. UPower combines that with the percentage and
   presents the battery as `empty`.

THE FIX
-------
Neither of the two invents data:

  - If the S2 average does not even reach the design minimum -that is, it is not
    saying anything credible- the ADC's last sample is used, which is
    instantaneous and valid from the very first moment. It is noisier, and that
    is why it is only used then.

  - The status is derived from the current, which the driver already reads and
    which already comes with the sign the API expects: positive when charging. A
    50 mA threshold so it does not dance between states with the idle noise.

WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED
--------------------
I have not reproduced the transient. Doing so requires a COMPLETE power-off, and
forcing one is exactly the risk this patch is trying to remove. What is measured
is every link in the chain: the two different registers, the average surviving a
reboot, the linear formula, and the unconditional UNKNOWN.

The pending check is to look at `capacity` and `status` the next time the phone
boots cold.

